the single biggest theme to pay  attention to right now in investing is  the federal reserve their monetary  policy and its impact on inflation we go  all the way back to 2020 we know that  the federal reserve pursued a very loose  monetary policy in response to the covet  impact that loose monetary policy drove  an asset bubble across all asset classes  and also we saw consumer prices explode  upwards that led to the 8.6  inflation that we have today it's a  40-year high it's mainly driven by that  loose monetary policy there was also  fiscal policy implemented by elected  officials there's supply chain  disruptions and a number of other things  that fed into it but loose monetary  policy was the main driver in my opinion  now going back about eight months the  federal reserve started to talk tough  they started to talk about the fact that  they were going to raise interest rates  and potentially conduct quantitative  tightening asset prices have started to  fall but inflation has been persistent  that's why we are at that 40-year high  today  recently on the all-in podcast chamath  palopita was talking about inflation cpi  being quite sticky around eight or nine  percent and why the federal reserve not  only are they not going to give up in  the short term in his opinion but it's  actually more likely that they're going  to over correct that they're going to  tighten so much that things will start  to break other assets that seem to be  unaffected today likely will feel the  impact of the federal reserve's  decisions here's chamas comments on the  all in podcast  i think you need to buckle your seat  belt because the next three four five  months of cpi  will probably be very very bad seven  eight nine percent why  there are a handful of components that  have gotten completely run away number  one the biggest one is rent and so rent  works on a three-month lag we're going  to reintroduce what the true  owner's equivalent rent is into cpi so  we can already forecast that cpi going  up oil is at 105 bucks a barrel russia  is basically trying to break the back of  europe by now messing with their nat gas  supplies um the german energy minister  yesterday said that if that happens it  could be a contagion equivalent to  lehman brothers with respect to energy  when you play all of these things out  what you have is unfortunately  rampant runaway costs that really have  no mechanism to get back in check  in the absence of some real governmental  changes our policy on this ukraine  russia war you know how we intend to  sort of uh work or cooperate or fight  with china all of these things have to  get solved so in the absence of that  prices are going to continue to go up  and so what does the fed do  how does it throw away what little  credibility it has left when there's  eight and nine percent inflation prints  and saying we think we're done for right  now you can't do that so they will over  correct because there is just going to  be so much  pressure for them to act all roads  i think lead to lower equity prices and  i think what david said astutely is  we've seen the first wave but now it has  to touch all these other areas now one  of the interesting things about the  federal reserve and their impact on  financial markets is that historically  they actually aren't worth paying  attention to what do i mean if you ask  somebody like peter lynch the famous  investor who beat the market for decades  he used to always say if you spent 16  minutes worrying about the macro economy  that's 14 minutes too long and just  focus on buying great companies focus on  just buying the businesses that will end  up generating more cash flow in the  future the problem is while there is  some truth to not paying attention to  the macro economy that the fed is in  control in the moment we don't have free  markets and the federal reserve has  created one big trait either you go with  the fed or you try to fight them and as  the old adage says don't fight the fed  the federal reserve conducting this  quantitative tightening and increasing  interest rates has created a correlation  closer and closer to one across all  assets historically the 6040 global  portfolio of stocks and bonds if stocks  go down bonds go up and vice versa but  instead what we've seen year to date in  2022 is that all assets are falling  stocks bonds currencies commodities real  estate bitcoin crypto and everything  else  as you see all assets falling together  it signals that we're in somewhat of a  liquidity crisis maybe shorter maybe  more drawn out nobody knows but at the  end of the day when all assets are  correlating towards one which you have  to ask yourself so what's the fed going  to do and until the fed reverses course  it's very unlikely that asset prices  will recover and the fed is only going  to reverse course if they cause so much  pain so much economic damage they were  sitting in a recession and people are  waving the white flag saying please give  up please give up  because until then the federal reserve's  just looking at that inflation number  and with it sitting at 8.6 percent in  may it's a big big problem and so  they're going to do everything they  possibly can go into their toolbox of  interest rates and quantitative  tightening and say we got to get  inflation under control this is the big  battle inflation versus the federal  reserve who will blink first we're going  to find out over the next six months or  so
 

